//api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/perses/perses/commit/d3f0c13a2cdf261e96cc4381ddb490b306a23c1f","node_id":"C_kwDOE9xoStoAKGQzZjBjMTNhMmNkZjI2MWU5NmNjNDM4MWRkYjQ5MGIzMDZhMjNjMWY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/perses/perses/commit/1274c92ee2bdc793847b611e0cbe9640f2e97dca","sha":"1274c92ee2bdc793847b611e0cbe9640f2e97dca","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/perses/perses/commits/1274c92ee2bdc793847b611e0cbe9640f2e97dca"}],"sha":"d3f0c13a2cdf261e96cc4381ddb490b306a23c1f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/perses/perses/commits/d3f0c13a2cdf261e96cc4381ddb490b306a23c1f"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4548045?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Nexucis/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Nexucis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Nexucis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Nexucis/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/Nexucis","id":4548045,"login":"Nexucis","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1NDgwNDU=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Nexucis/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Nexucis/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Nexucis/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Nexucis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Nexucis/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Nexucis","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/perses/perses/commits/1274c92ee2bdc793847b611e0cbe9640f2e97dca/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-05T15:39:38Z","email":"husson.augustin@gmail.com","name":"Augustin Husson"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-05T15:39:38Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"[ENHANCEMENT] Native authorization: build the permission cache in linear time and deduplicate role binding subjects (#4540)\n\n* [IGNORE] Add benchmarks for the native authorization\n\nAdd benchmarks covering the native authorization on 3 dataset sizes\n(100 users / 10 projects up to 10,000 users / 1,000 projects):\n- the cache rebuild (RefreshPermissionsAndRoles), with one or several\n  concurrent callers,\n- HasPermission, GetUserProjects and GetPermissions, for global admins,\n  global viewers, project members, unknown users and claim-based users,\n- the claim-based permission resolution and the core matching function,\n- HasPermission under concurrent requests, with and without a concurrent\n  cache refresh.\n\nThe fake DAOs and the dataset generator live in fixtures_test.go so that\nthey can be shared with the unit tests.\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>\n\n* [IGNORE] Benchmark 25 simultaneous first logins on the native authorization\n\nBenchmarkConcurrentRefreshPermissionsAndRoles now simulates 25 OAuth/OIDC\nusers logging in for the first time at the same time, each one triggering a\nsynchronous refresh of the permission cache. The time per operation is the\nwait of the slowest login: it would be 25 times the time of a single refresh\nif the refreshes were serialized.\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>\n\n* [ENHANCEMENT] Native authorization: build the permission cache in linear time\n\nThe permission cache was built by iterating over every role binding for\nevery user, and by looking up the role of each binding linearly: the\ncomplexity was O(users × role bindings × subjects). With 10,000 users and\n1,000 projects, a refresh took 2.8 s. As every first login of an OAuth/OIDC\nuser triggers a synchronous refresh, 25 simultaneous first logins made the\nslowest one wait 13 s.\n\nThe roles are now indexed, and the subjects of each binding are iterated\nonce: the complexity is linear in the number of users, roles, binding\nsubjects and granted permissions. A refresh now takes ~20 ms with 10,000\nusers and 1,000 projects.\n\nThe result is unchanged: same users, projects and permissions, in the same\norder. It is checked against the previous algorithm, kept in the tests as a\nreference.\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>\n\n* [ENHANCEMENT] Native authorization: remove duplicated subjects when unmarshalling role bindings\n\nThe subjects of a role binding are now deduplicated when it is unmarshalled\n(JSON or YAML), keeping the first occurrence of each subject and their order.\nAs the bindings are unmarshalled when they are created, updated or read from\nthe database, a subject appears only once in a binding. buildUsersPermissions\ndoesn''t need to track the subjects already found in the current binding\nanymore: it only checks that a subject is an existing user.\n\nThe role of a binding is also resolved before iterating over its subjects:\na binding referencing an unknown role is ignored without iterating over its\nsubjects, and is now logged once, even if none of its subjects is an existing\nuser.\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>\n\n* [ENHANCEMENT] Role bindings: cheaper deduplication of the subjects, and documentation\n\n- The small subject lists (up to 16 subjects) are deduplicated by scanning\n  the subjects already kept, without allocating a map: deduplicating the\n  subjects of a role binding of 10 subjects doesn''t cost any extra memory or\n  allocation anymore.\n- Document that the duplicated subjects are removed (API documentation),\n  what happens if a binding reaches the cache without being unmarshalled,\n  and why the cache build still ignores the subjects that are not users.\n- Clarify the names of the unmarshalling tests, and test a list of subjects\n  larger than the linear scan threshold.\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>","tree":{"sha":"99001aeacf04d8660dd79bb28e6a35a5cf6e0ef4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/perses/perses/git/trees/99001aeacf04d8660dd79bb28e6a35a5cf6e0ef4"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/perses/perses/git/commits/1274c92ee2bdc793847b611e0cbe9640f2e97dca","verification":{"payload":"tree 99001aeacf04d8660dd79bb28e6a35a5cf6e0ef4\nparent 917b89fae659cdc02d4462c772d7ebacc0e5ca66\nauthor Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com> 1791214778 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791214778 +0200\n\n[ENHANCEMENT] Native authorization: build the permission cache in linear time and deduplicate role binding subjects (#4540)\n\n* [IGNORE] Add benchmarks for the native authorization\n\nAdd benchmarks covering the native authorization on 3 dataset sizes\n(100 users / 10 projects up to 10,000 users / 1,000 projects):\n- the cache rebuild (RefreshPermissionsAndRoles), with one or several\n  concurrent callers,\n- HasPermission, GetUserProjects and GetPermissions, for global admins,\n  global viewers, project members, unknown users and claim-based users,\n- the claim-based permission resolution and the core matching function,\n- HasPermission under concurrent requests, with and without a concurrent\n  cache refresh.\n\nThe fake DAOs and the dataset generator live in fixtures_test.go so that\nthey can be shared with the unit tests.\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>\n\n* [IGNORE] Benchmark 25 simultaneous first logins on the native authorization\n\nBenchmarkConcurrentRefreshPermissionsAndRoles now simulates 25 OAuth/OIDC\nusers logging in for the first time at the same time, each one triggering a\nsynchronous refresh of the permission cache. The time per operation is the\nwait of the slowest login: it would be 25 times the time of a single refresh\nif the refreshes were serialized.\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>\n\n* [ENHANCEMENT] Native authorization: build the permission cache in linear time\n\nThe permission cache was built by iterating over every role binding for\nevery user, and by looking up the role of each binding linearly: the\ncomplexity was O(users × role bindings × subjects). With 10,000 users and\n1,000 projects, a refresh took 2.8 s. As every first login of an OAuth/OIDC\nuser triggers a synchronous refresh, 25 simultaneous first logins made the\nslowest one wait 13 s.\n\nThe roles are now indexed, and the subjects of each binding are iterated\nonce: the complexity is linear in the number of users, roles, binding\nsubjects and granted permissions. A refresh now takes ~20 ms with 10,000\nusers and 1,000 projects.\n\nThe result is unchanged: same users, projects and permissions, in the same\norder. It is checked against the previous algorithm, kept in the tests as a\nreference.\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>\n\n* [ENHANCEMENT] Native authorization: remove duplicated subjects when unmarshalling role bindings\n\nThe subjects of a role binding are now deduplicated when it is unmarshalled\n(JSON or YAML), keeping the first occurrence of each subject and their order.\nAs the bindings are unmarshalled when they are created, updated or read from\nthe database, a subject appears only once in a binding. buildUsersPermissions\ndoesn''t need to track the subjects already found in the current binding\nanymore: it only checks that a subject is an existing user.\n\nThe role of a binding is also resolved before iterating over its subjects:\na binding referencing an unknown role is ignored without iterating over its\nsubjects, and is now logged once, even if none of its subjects is an existing\nuser.\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>\n\n* [ENHANCEMENT] Role bindings: cheaper deduplication of the subjects, and documentation\n\n- The small subject lists (up to 16 subjects) are deduplicated by scanning\n  the subjects already kept, without allocating a map: deduplicating the\n  subjects of a role binding of 10 subjects doesn''t cost any extra memory or\n  allocation anymore.\n- Document that the duplicated subjects are removed (API documentation),\n  what happens if a binding reaches the cache without being unmarshalled,\n  and why the cache build still ignores the subjects that are not users.\n- Clarify the names of the unmarshalling tests, and test a list of subjects\n  larger than the linear scan threshold.\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqw8S6CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAZGIQAFHe0tk/btPe6cH1VFh8ZjV4\n+QS+kn0d8fqqS1PKmXNOGaRXDWJY2h2gQ+reg6UhFi2m51iVzU0joo2RKXzBDK9C\nv4VVAHyqR+btJITrVL6tSSAwkUlVhFkC1QGHJrGs/rQ39pnWWCSSxpxXRBWqXdym\ngZELZAw8T3pWr5JfeeWGQ2WvvuUMp+2URaPJsGNzvJDPSe8S7sUQKNBDyV0oGBJo\n5nMGtYozby25cqRjyQuRyfYI4ezqWZChqw6XtQdiovoO5hsChr+QZbtXquYQbUv2\n2H+h0Cp2hPI0z15uIV70yJfjqb+SFyv4T5jwgmMruVmVaHHCWsgDgr4L//HtRqer\n8kOdIkCPtR+Th0rZgPKImYZpCfQ/b9N5GplD0gm3mLyFtYz1McwP6MLJAVl9dYDs\nraHyGfx25SWAlalx8gqt6b1/W5pVO0pEMKZgE981g3APcCbnRvvJ8I5g1gF3zDGP\nhrYPIGzfFdtfMda8IXF6dAbNfP6zaoWyMXrC5/mOanDBL8rq2rqPtIjgQbFOhP/b\nElR7bJQJnSIVqV6Tcx3v4t+sJd3jLY4IIwRLbqcGymEnYiRFqxXR3LUqgydyFJLK\nbgnAXTtAS4lWQM5dxFy28tljJI33IYTjokLf0bsWA44EBQz/FLKBdAgBk1AdqWFt\nsCEZEO9IP9BmcyTWlewG\n=UM+3\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-05T15:39:39Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.g