s/perses/commit/379673eb0e1198cb21536fae72e2d334d7d6a6a1","sha":"379673eb0e1198cb21536fae72e2d334d7d6a6a1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/perses/perses/commits/379673eb0e1198cb21536fae72e2d334d7d6a6a1"}],"sha":"6fc0c58510c0543627856ea708c28f11c9e8a765","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/perses/perses/commits/6fc0c58510c0543627856ea708c28f11c9e8a765"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4548045?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Nexucis/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Nexucis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Nexucis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Nexucis/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/Nexucis","id":4548045,"login":"Nexucis","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1NDgwNDU=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Nexucis/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Nexucis/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Nexucis/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Nexucis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Nexucis/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Nexucis","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/perses/perses/commits/379673eb0e1198cb21536fae72e2d334d7d6a6a1/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-29T14:47:05Z","email":"husson.augustin@gmail.com","name":"Augustin Husson"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-29T14:47:05Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"[BUGFIX] SQL proxy: fix TLS handling, read-only enforcement and driver options (#4522)\n\n* [BUGFIX] SQL proxy: fix TLS handling, read-only enforcement and driver options\n\n- MySQL/MariaDB: set the TLS config on the connection config instead of the global registry of the driver, where concurrent requests to datasources registering the same name could use the TLS config of each other. Start from the default config of the driver (mysql_native_password allowed). Always disable multiStatements. Apply the dial timeout.\n- PostgreSQL: don''t force a TLS config when the secret doesn''t define one, so the sslMode is respected. Set the server name from the host when using the TLS config of the secret. Convert connectTimeout to seconds, ignore prepareThreshold (JDBC option rejected by the server), apply maxConns to the database/sql pool.\n- Execute the queries in a read-only transaction, and only accept queries starting with a read statement keyword, as the previous blocklist could be bypassed (data-modifying CTE, DO, CALL, RENAME, ...).\n- Fix the SQL proxy field names in the documentation.\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>\n\n* [BUGFIX] SQL proxy: always disable the unsafe MySQL driver options\n\nThe params of a MySQL/MariaDB datasource are interpreted like the params of a DSN,\nso they could enable options of the driver letting the server of the datasource:\n- read any file of the Perses server (allowAllFiles): the driver sends the file requested\n  by the server with LOAD DATA LOCAL INFILE, and the server can request it in response to any query.\n  Anyone able to query an unsaved datasource could point it at a server they control.\n- get the password of the datasource in clear text (allowCleartextPasswords),\n  or authenticate with the insecure old password method (allowOldPasswords).\n\nThese options are now always disabled, whatever the params say, like multiStatements.\nAs a consequence, the authentication methods requiring the password in clear text\n(mysql_clear_password, e.g. PAM or LDAP) are not supported.\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>\n\n* [BUGFIX] SQL proxy: explicit error when the database doesn''t support read-only transactions, and clarify the docs\n\n- Every query is executed in a read-only transaction. A database only compatible with the MySQL or PostgreSQL\n  protocol may not support it: every query then failed with a generic internal error.\n  The connection is now opened explicitly before starting the transaction, so the errors to connect can be told apart\n  from the ones to start the transaction. The latter are now reported with an explicit message.\n- Document that the database must support read-only transactions, and the side effects that a read-only transaction\n  doesn''t prevent (e.g. SELECT ... INTO OUTFILE, administration functions, dblink_exec).\n- Document that, with a TLS config in the secret, the certificate of a PostgreSQL server is always verified with it,\n  including its hostname: unlike libpq, ''require'' and ''verify-ca'' behave like ''verify-full'', unless insecureSkipVerify is set.\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>\n\n* simplify defer function\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>","tree":{"sha":"da1594a95acaa1863931090309ab0793d74fd0d9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/perses/perses/git/trees/da1594a95acaa1863931090309ab0793d74fd0d9"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/perses/perses/git/commits/379673eb0e1198cb21536fae72e2d334d7d6a6a1","verification":{"payload":"tree da1594a95acaa1863931090309ab0793d74fd0d9\nparent bb4a7c03870d553de7dcbe9e6b22d978f7f233d2\nauthor Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com> 1790693225 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790693225 +0000\n\n[BUGFIX] SQL proxy: fix TLS handling, read-only enforcement and driver options (#4522)\n\n* [BUGFIX] SQL proxy: fix TLS handling, read-only enforcement and driver options\n\n- MySQL/MariaDB: set the TLS config on the connection config instead of the global registry of the driver, where concurrent requests to datasources registering the same name could use the TLS config of each other. Start from the default config of the driver (mysql_native_password allowed). Always disable multiStatements. Apply the dial timeout.\n- PostgreSQL: don''t force a TLS config when the secret doesn''t define one, so the sslMode is respected. Set the server name from the host when using the TLS config of the secret. Convert connectTimeout to seconds, ignore prepareThreshold (JDBC option rejected by the server), apply maxConns to the database/sql pool.\n- Execute the queries in a read-only transaction, and only accept queries starting with a read statement keyword, as the previous blocklist could be bypassed (data-modifying CTE, DO, CALL, RENAME, ...).\n- Fix the SQL proxy field names in the documentation.\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>\n\n* [BUGFIX] SQL proxy: always disable the unsafe MySQL driver options\n\nThe params of a MySQL/MariaDB datasource are interpreted like the params of a DSN,\nso they could enable options of the driver letting the server of the datasource:\n- read any file of the Perses server (allowAllFiles): the driver sends the file requested\n  by the server with LOAD DATA LOCAL INFILE, and the server can request it in response to any query.\n  Anyone able to query an unsaved datasource could point it at a server they control.\n- get the password of the datasource in clear text (allowCleartextPasswords),\n  or authenticate with the insecure old password method (allowOldPasswords).\n\nThese options are now always disabled, whatever the params say, like multiStatements.\nAs a consequence, the authentication methods requiring the password in clear text\n(mysql_clear_password, e.g. PAM or LDAP) are not supported.\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>\n\n* [BUGFIX] SQL proxy: explicit error when the database doesn''t support read-only transactions, and clarify the docs\n\n- Every query is executed in a read-only transaction. A database only compatible with the MySQL or PostgreSQL\n  protocol may not support it: every query then failed with a generic internal error.\n  The connection is now opened explicitly before starting the transaction, so the errors to connect can be told apart\n  from the ones to start the transaction. The latter are now reported with an explicit message.\n- Document that the database must support read-only transactions, and the side effects that a read-only transaction\n  doesn''t prevent (e.g. SELECT ... INTO OUTFILE, administration functions, dblink_exec).\n- Document that, with a TLS config in the secret, the certificate of a PostgreSQL server is always verified with it,\n  including its hostname: unlike libpq, ''require'' and ''verify-ca'' behave like ''verify-full'', unless insecureSkipVerify is set.\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>\n\n* simplify defer function\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Augustin Husson <husson.augustin@gmail.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqu895CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAAcsQAKC7MHES4wYrNPKkM1riQIKh\n2ljjHJ3hhtystWSMH2fmJh51O5OKDjIIn/Y8XiCPXP1x49j39WwkAH7AzBktZ1BY\nUYNXjB16Yj8KiL//4OL5nE9eZepmP0z0UEs/ZIA25Ut5kClsoLzcvjqD9Q19JvIx\nNy60gXlYEe0FwONEaLFBcW4CQmxDw4u0PE1th9zhY0hj3e2FgUpH6UtXGqaOI8ZG\n62x+19HttwcgS4c8MnWG2TCSAI+3sB+kdUK/64rD4QywWrqRIYUiyl0mjXxh1zUM\nd0a68ZuMsluaAdIcMmVU4i7ZVn8zA9fyefE1X+L3d83fEc6Z3QmT+T9AoxueLOzd\niN1xaUawSELY/NrbQaThOBVVLHpoVUBzR26nc/358CtOY+vbcxVrheDMDf2//KgD\n5huzGQs7Ya5yA9qWEciZ53aZed8RKs+kmfkGkv0yuuYnKLJZziA9BGhCiMDEslQt\npuFKGqS/19IBhV9PrVY3K8mRkY/XZZ6YIx2OvSWC9UCrCUIt1SPuPbxo0eRmtA5A\nNETLphruLyVXQr0CgWkUjuRLICzPIiEJ2K67o6fjtYmd6q5RYhHUX5HEsTUOMy1X\nRBiNfQdJLmoO+nmdgwerdvYoN1938rWadPfZJ3IiPiZw2LI3VYPY6C0eRS79fwbR\nJL3LCUhX8y2H1I9I+a3L\n=ILXo\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-29T14:47:21Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following